2026-09-16T15:02:53.971050Z [] {'tender_id': None, 'source_reference_id': None, 'source_id': None, 'title': None, 'issuing_agency': None, 'category': None, 'status': None, 'publish_date': None, 'closing_date': None, 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': None, 'value_notes': None, 'location': None, 'description': '', 'contact_name': None, 'contact_email': None, 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': None, 'documents': [{'file_name': 'DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 - Addendum 1.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \nDepartment of Defence \n(ABN 68706814312) \n \n \nRequest for Tender  \n \nEV Charging Management System (CMS) \nRFT DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 \n \nAddendum Number 1 \n\nADDENDUM NO. 1 \n \nQuestions and Answers: \n \nQuestion 1: Can Defence confirm the required Tender submission structure, \nincluding which Conditions of Tender annexes and draft Contract attachments \nTenderers must completed and returned? \nAnswer 1: Tenderers should structure their submission to include the completed \nreturnable schedules, forms and information requested in the Request documentation. \nTenderers should refer to the tender documents, particularly the Conditions of Tender \nand Annexes and the Draft Conditions of Contract and Attachments. \nQuestion 2: Can Defence confirm whether the scope of this procurement includes the \nphysical installation, commissioning or repair of Electric Vehicle chargers or \nassociated electrical infrastructure? \nAnswer 2: The scope of this procurement does not include the physical installation, \nmaintenance or repair of Electric Vehicle chargers, or other supporting electrical \ninfrastructure. Tenderers should refer to Section 3.1 of Attachment A to the draft \nConditions of Contract, for the specific scope inclusions.  \nQuestion 3: Can Defence confirm whether certification to SOC 2 Type II and/or \nISO/IEC 27001 is a mandatory requirement of the tender, or whether equivalent \nevidence of information security controls and governance arrangements will be \nconsidered acceptable? \nAnswer 3: Tenderers are advised that certification to SOC 2 Type II and/or ISO/IEC \n27001 is a mandatory requirement of this procurement.  \n \nAccreditation is intended to provide assurance that the Tenderer?s information \nsecurity management systems, policies and controls have been independently assessed \nagainst a recognized industry standard. Tenderers are expected to demonstrate that the \nproposed CMS solution and associated service delivery arrangements are covered by \na current SOC2 Type II attestation and/or ISO/IEC 27001 certification.  \n \n \n \n------------END OF ADDENDUM NO. 1------------- \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 - Addendum 2.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \nDepartment of Defence \n(ABN 68706814312) \n \n \nRequest for Tender  \n \nEV Charging Management System (CMS) \nRFT DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 \n \nAddendum Number 2 \n\nADDENDUM NO. 2 \n \nQuestions and Answers: \n \nQuestion 1: Does the tenderer need to hold SOC 2 or ISO 27001 certification? How \ndo these requirements apply where the CMS platform is provided by a subcontractor? \nAnswer 1: Tenderers holding either SOC 2 or ISO 27001 certification by the Contract \nCommencement Date should consider themselves eligible to submit a response. \nWhere a tenderer proposes using a subcontractor or third-party software provider, \nDefence?s primary concern is that the CMS platform and associated service \nenvironment are supported by an appropriate information security management \nframework. Accordingly, the subcontractor or software provider must hold SOC 2 \nand/or ISO 27001 certification. While it is preferable for the head tenderer to also \nhold SOC2 and/or ISO 27001 certification, this is not a mandatory requirement.  \nSimilarly, where requirements reference alignment with OWASP security practices, \nDefence?s primary concern is that the CMS platform itself demonstrates compliance \nwith the relevant requirements.  \nQuestion 2: How many charging points are expected to be connected to the CMS at \ncontract commencement? What constitutes a charging point and what is the expected \nbreakdown of charger types? \nAnswer 2: At contract commencement, Defence expects approximately 40 charging \npoints to be online and equipped with the necessary communications infrastructure to \nenable connection to the CMS.  \nFor the purpose of this procurement, a charging point refers to an individual charging \nconnector (plug). Where a charger contains multiple connectors, each connector \nshould be considered a separate charging point. For example, a duel-socket charger \nwould be considered two charging points.  \nOf the charging points expected to be connected at contract commencement: \n? 2 charging points are Mode 4 \n? The remainder are Mode 3 \nTenderers should note that additional charging infrastructure is being delivered under \nseveral Defence projects. The total number of charging points, as well as the number \nof Mode 4 charging points, is expected to increase throughout FY2026-27 and \nbeyond. Refer to the SoW for forecasted growth in charging points across the estate. \n\nExisting charging infrastructure comprises equipment from a range of charger \nhardware and firmware suppliers. Detailed information such as charger models, \nfirmware and configurations will only be made available to the successful tenderer \nfollowing contract award.  \nQuestion 3: Are the existing EV chargers currently installed across the Defence estate \nconnected to a central point (e.g., a site-level network switch, gateway, or head-end \ndevice) via physical network cabling, or do they currently operate without any such \nwired network connection to a central point? \nAnswer 3: All charge points ready for CMS connection are fitted with the standard \nconnectivity solution, including the hardware and telecommunications components \noutlined in the EV Charging Guideline. \nQuestion 4: What level of onsite attendance is anticipated under Workstream 3 for \ncommissioning activities and fault identification/remediation? \nAnswer 4: Defence anticipates the requirement for onsite attendance under \nWorkstream 3 will be limited. Following successful pilot implementations and \nestablishment of repeatable commissioning processes for new chargers, Defence \nexpects the majority of commissioning activities to be performed remotely. This may \ninclude:  \n? Verification of charger connectivity to the CMS \n? Confirmation of successful communications and data exchange \n? Remote configuration and validation activities \n? Coordination with Defence project delivery teams. \nSimilarly, Defence expects fault investigation activities to primarily consist of remote \ndiagnostics, monitoring and troubleshooting. Where physical inspection, repair or \nsite-based intervention is required, tenderers should assume Defence?s existing \nmaintenance and support arrangements will generally be responsible for undertaking \nonsite activities under the direction of Defence.  \nTenderers are not expected to maintain a broad Australia-wide field service capability \nas part of the CMS services contemplated in this procurement. \nQuestion 5: Are tenderers required to hold or attain Defence Industry Security \nProgram (DISP) accreditation or seek security clearances for personnel if successful? \nAnswer 5. Refer to Conditions of Contract Information Table Item 6. Tenderers are \nnot required to hold DISP accreditation to participate in this procurement. The \nsuccessful tenderer is not required to obtain DISP accreditation prior to or during \ndelivery.  \n\nPersonnel proposed by tenderers are not required to hold a security clearance at the \ntime of tender submission. However, Defence?s preference is that nominated \npersonnel are Australian citizens and are eligible to apply for a Baseline Security \nClearance should this become necessary during contract delivery. \nTenderers seeking further information regarding Australian Government security \nclearances should refer to the Australian Government Security Vetting Agency \n(AGSVA) guidance materials.  \n------------END OF ADDENDUM NO. 2------------- \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 - Addendum 3.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \nDepartment of Defence \n(ABN 68706814312) \n \n \nRequest for Tender  \n \nEV Charging Management System (CMS) \nRFT DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 \n \nAddendum Number 3 \n\nADDENDUM NO. 3 \nTenderers are advised of the following amendment to the Tender Documents: \n \n1. Amendment to closing date and time for the Request for Tender from 4:30pm \n28 August 2026 to 12pm (ACT time) 18 September 2026. \na. Following consideration by the Commonwealth, the closing date for this \nRequest for Tender has been extended to 12pm (ACT time) 18 \nSeptember 2026. \n \n \n \n------------END OF ADDENDUM NO. 3------------- \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 - Addendum 4.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \nDepartment of Defence \n(ABN 68706814312) \n \n \nRequest for Tender  \n \nEV Charging Management System (CMS) \nRFT DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 \n \nAddendum Number 4 \n\nADDENDUM NO. 4 \n \nQuestions and Answers: \n \nQuestion 1: What categories of users may access EV charging infrastructure on the \nDefence estate, and how should they be managed within the CMS? \nAnswer 1: For a detailed description of user categories and charging policies, refer to \nthe Electric Vehicle Charging Guideline at Annex 2. \nFor CMS purposes, Defence?s primary distinction is between:  \n? Fleet vehicle users (Defence fleet vehicles); and \n? Non-fleet vehicle users (including private vehicles belonging to ADF members, \ncontractors and visitors). \nMany chargers across the Defence estate are designated for fleet vehicle use only. \nFleet users are expected to authenticate using RFID cards. The CMS is not expected \nto facilitate payment collection for fleet vehicle charging sessions. However, the CMS \nmust be capable of providing charging session data to Defence?s fleet management \nplatform to facilitate internal reporting and cost allocation.  \nSome chargers are accessible to both fleet and non-fleet users. Non-fleet users will be \nrequired to authenticate using a supported authentication method linked to a valid \npayment mechanism. The CMS must facilitate charging fee collection for non-fleet \ncharging sessions.  \nAt this stage, Defence does not require the CMS to differentiate between \nsubcategories of non-fleet users (e.g. ADF personnel, contractors, visitors). However, \nthe ability to support differentiated user groups, permissions and pricing structure in \nthe future is desirable.  \nQuestion 2: What authentication must the CMS support and who is responsible for \nadministering them? \nAnswer 2: The CMS must support the following authentication method:  \n1. RFID card authentication for Defence fleet vehicles. \n2. Non-fleet user authentication that verifies the user has access to a valid payment \nmethod prior to commencing a charging session.  \nSuitable non-fleet authentication methods may include:  \n? Mobile application or web portal login; \n? Single Sign-On mechanisms; \n\n? Credit card or digital wallet payment workflows; or \n? Other equivalent approaches proposed by the tenderer. \nThe successful tenderer will be responsible for providing, administering, supporting \nand maintaining the authentication mechanisms proposed as part of their solution. \nDefence will support activities that require integration with existing Defence-\nmanaged systems or datasets, such as existing authorised RFID card lists and \npermissions.  \nQuestion 3: Does Defence require the CMS to be accessible via both a web-based \nportal and a native mobile application? \nAnswer 3: Defence requires end users to have convenient access to CMS \nfunctionality from mobile devices. A responsive, mobile-friendly website providing \nequivalent functionality to a native application is acceptable. However, a native \nmobile application for end users is preferred. For Defence administrators, a desktop or \nlaptop-based administration portal is preferred. \nQuestion 4: What payment model is required for non-fleet vehicle charging? \nAnswer 4: Defence is open to tenderers proposing their preferred payment collection \napproach for non-fleet charging. Defence?s preference is for software-based payment \nprocessing integrated with a third-party payment gateway, subject to Defence \napproval. However, alternative approaches may also be proposed, including physical \npayment hardware.  \nWhere physical payment infrastructure forms part of the proposed solution, the \nsuccessful tenderer will be responsible for supplying and implementing that \ncapability. \nQuestion 5: What payment processing and funds collection model will apply? \nAnswer 5: The successful tenderer must provide and operate its own merchant or \npayment account and collect charging proceeds. The mechanism for transfer of those \nfunds to Defence will be negotiated during contract finalisation. \nQuestion 6: How will charging fees and tariffs be determined? \nAnswer 6: Defence will retain responsibility for setting charging fees and tariff \npolicies. Tenderers are welcome to provide recommendations regarding pricing \napproaches for Defence?s consideration.  \n \n \n\nQuestion 7: Which interfaces are expected to comply with WCAG accessibility \nrequirements? \nAnswer 7: Defence?s preference is that all end-user facing interfaces achieve WCAG \n2.0 Level AA compliance or higher. Defence does not require formal accessibility \ncertification or conformance evidence to be submitted as part of the tender response. \nHowever, tenderers are encouraged to provide evidence if available. \nQuestion 8: Must the CMS integrate with a Defence-nominated identity provider? \nAnswer 8: No. The CMS platform itself is not required to integrate with Defence-\nnominated identity provider for end-user authentication or administration purposes.  \nHowever, Defence requires that personnel supporting the management and operation \nof Defence?s charger connectivity solution (refer to Workstream B (ii) of the \nStatement of Work) use Defence?s nominated identity provider for access. Tenderers \nshould therefore assume that specific support personnel may be required to \nauthenticate via Defence-managed identity services when performing connectivity \nmanagement activities on behalf of Defence.  \n \n------------END OF ADDENDUM NO. 4------------- \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 - Addendum 5.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \nDepartment of Defence \n(ABN 68706814312) \n \n \nRequest for Tender  \n \nEV Charging Management System (CMS) \nRFT DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 \n \nAddendum Number 5 \n\nADDENDUM NO. 5 \nIn accordance with clause Conditions of Tender clause 1.2.1, of DCET-EVCI-CMS-\n2026, Defence provides the following amendments to the RFT documentation and \nresponses to the following Tenderer questions. \n \n \nAmendments to RFT Documentation \n \nAttachment A to the Conditions of Contract, clause 3.1(c)(vii) \n \nExisting clause: Liaise with live Defence projects that will be installing EV charging \npoints to facilitate connection to the CMS (refer to Annex 5) \n \n \nRevised clause: Liaise with live Defence projects that will be installing EV charging \npoints to facilitate connection to the CMS (refer to Annex 1) \n \n \nQuestions and Answers: \n \nQuestion 1: Should Scope of Work clause 3.1(c)(vii) refer to Annex 5, and is \nAttachment E missing from the Conditions of Contract? \nAnswer 1: Scope of Work clause 3.1(c)(vii) should refer to Annex 1. There is no \nAnnex 5 relevant to this procurement. Similarly, Attachment E to the Conditions of \nContract is not applicable to this procurement and has not been omitted in error. Only \nAttachments A to D and Attachment F apply.  \nQuestion 2: What is the intent of the protocol and interoperability requirements in \nAnnex 1, Requirement 7, and when must this capability be available? \nAnswer 2: The intent of this requirement is to ensure the CMS can interoperate with \ndedicated on-site hardware supporting EV charging load management and energy \nmonitoring (e.g. site meters, local load management systems, and protocol gateways).  \nDefence?s baseline expectation is that essential load management (enforcement of site \ncapacity limits) will be performed locally at the majority of EV charging sites, using \nan OCPP-enabled load management device supported by upstream metering where \nrequired. Direct communication between the CMS and other local energy monitoring \nhardware using the hardware?s native communication protocols (e.g. Modbus \nTCP/RTU, OPC UA, EtherNet/IP) is not a mandatory requirement. However, \nTenderer?s whose CMS can additionally support dynamic, cloud-coordinated load \noptimization, whilst preserving local fail-safe capability, are preferred, and should \ndescribe this capability and any roadmap to it. Tenderers may demonstrate this \ncapability through an approved on-site gateway, controller or other integration layer \nthat securely exchanges data with the CMS.  \n\nQuestion 3: How many edge devices are currently used for the Defence EV Charging \nWAN, and what WAN documentation and responsibilities will transfer to the \nsuccessful the successful Tenderer? \nAnswer 3: Under Annex 1, Requirement 13, the successful Tenderer will be \nresponsible for operating and maintaining the dedicated Defence EV charging Wide \nArea Network. The number of routers connected to the EV charging WAN is \nexpected to increase from approximately 10 at contract commencement to \napproximately 50 by FY2031-32. These figures are indicative only. The actual \nnumber of endpoints and rate of growth will depend on the timing and scale of \nDefence?s EV charging infrastructure rollout.  \nRefer to Appendix A of Guideline 8000 for details of the edge devices used across \nDefence EV charging installations, including relevant manufacturers and models.  \nThe Defence EV Charging WAN is currently managed internally by Defence, with \ntechnical assistance obtained from hardware supply partners where required. \nDefence?s existing documentation covering the management and operation of the \nWAN will be provided to the successful Tenderer. This includes access and \nadministration, network and device configuration, and new device setup and \nonboarding.  \nQuestion 4: What integration is required with Defence systems for fleet billing and \ninternal cost recovery under Annex 1, Requirement 6 and what data constraints apply? \nAnswer 4: The CMS must support integration with Defence-approved systems for \nfleet billing and internal cost recovery. Defence will provide the successful Tenderer \nwith further information regarding the relevant Defence fleet management platform. \nDefence?s preferred integration is an automated export or transfer of CMS charging-\nsession data to the Defence fleet management platform. The exact format and \nfrequency for this data transfer will be agreed with the successful Tenderer during \nimplementation.  \nThe relevant data constraints for the integration apply only to Defence fleet vehicles. \nDefence intends to maintain separation between the CMS and the detailed fleet \nrecords held within Defence?s fleet management and billing system. For fleet \ncharging sessions, it is expected that the CMS will record the Defence-issued RFID \ncard used to initiate the transaction. However, within the CMS, that identifier will not \nbe mapped to the vehicle registration or other fleet information, such as the \nresponsible unit, ownership or cost centre. This information will remain within \nDefence?s fleet management platform only. Internal fleet billing and cost recovery \nwill be performed within that system, rather than the CMS.  \n\nThe integration should therefore enable CMS charging-session data to be matched \nwith Defence fleet vehicle records based on Defence-issued RFID cards used to \ninitiate charging sessions. The exported data should include the relevant RFID \nidentifier and sufficient session information to support reconciliation. This may \ninclude but is not limited to; charger ID, location, session date and time, and energy \nconsumption. Defence are open to Tenderers proposing alternative implementations \nthat meet these security requirements, such as pseudonymised or tokenized identifiers \nfor fleet vehicles.  \nThese specific data separation requirements do not apply to non-fleet vehicles or \nusers. However, the successful Tenderer remains responsible for managing all non-\nfleet user and payment information in accordance with applicable privacy and security \nrequirements. \nTesting of the fleet vehicle system integration will be scoped and undertaken under \nWork stream 1, once the system details and integration arrangements have been \nprovided to the successful Tenderer. \n \n------------END OF ADDENDUM NO. 5 ------------- \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 - Addendum 6.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \n \n \n \nDepartment of Defence \n(ABN 68706814312) \n \n \nRequest for Tender  \n \nEV Charging Management System (CMS) \nRFT DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 \n \nAddendum Number 6 \n\nADDENDUM NO. 6 \n \nQuestions and Answers: \n \nQuestion 1: Does a logically isolated SaaS environment hosted in an Australian \nregion of a public cloud platform satisfy Defence?s cloud-hosting requirements? \nAnswer 1: Yes. Defence will accept a solution hosted in an Australian region of a \npublic cloud platform, provided it maintains Australian data residency and \ndemonstrates appropriate logical separation, segregation controls and independent \nsecurity assurance. \nQuestion 2: What are the commercial and operational responsibilities for the \ndedicated EV charging WAN? \nAnswer 2: Defence holds the existing subscriptions and licenses for the WAN. \nLicence and subscription costs will be funded by Defence, either directly or as a pass-\nthrough cost. \nFor new charging points delivered by Defence projects, installation contractors will \ncontinue to procure and install the connectivity hardware, SIM cards and \nsubscriptions in accordance with the applicable Defence requirements. The successful \ntenderer will assume operational management when the charging points are connected \nto the CMS. \nThe successful tenderer is not required to establish a direct commercial relationship \nwith the router supplier or SIM carrier, but may need to communicate operationally \nwith those suppliers. The dedicated EV charging WAN comprises the network \ninfrastructure providing connectivity between the charging points and the CMS. \nQuestion 3: What remote-access arrangements apply to personnel supporting the \nconnectivity solution? \nAnswer 3: The remote-access client may be downloaded free of charge and installed \non provider-managed endpoints. The successful tenderer will require user licences for \npersonnel accessing the SASE ZTNA environment. Defence will coordinate the \nrequired licences with the relevant supplier, with the costs either paid directly by \nDefence or treated as pass-through costs. \nQuestion 4: Does Specification requirement 1 require the CMS to be controllable by \nseparate third-party software? \nAnswer 4: No. The requirement means that the CMS must be capable of controlling \nthe relevant hardware and software components within the solution. The CMS is the \nthird-party software referred to in the requirement. It is not required to be controllable \nby a separate third-party platform. \n\n \nQuestion 5: Is the successful tenderer responsible for procuring and physically \nmanaging RFID cards? \nAnswer 5: The successful tenderer will not be responsible for physically managing \nRFID cards used for Defence fleet vehicles. Commercial fuel cards managed by \nDefence?s fleet service provider will be the primary RFID cards used by fleet \nvehicles. The successful tenderer must work with Defence?s fleet manager to \nadminister the associated CMS register and charging permissions, including \npermissions for spare RFID cards held at Defence sites. \nIf the successful tenderer proposes RFID cards for non-fleet users, it will be \nresponsible for the physical management of those cards. \nQuestion 6: What is the scope of Work stream 1, including the number of sites and \ncharging points and the applicable acceptance requirements? \nAnswer 6: At contract commencement, Defence expects approximately 40 \nconnection-ready charging points across approximately 10 sites. Sites have an average \nof four charging points, with the number at individual sites generally ranging from \ntwo to eight. \nThe approximately 40 connection-ready charging points are to be connected and \naccepted under Work stream 1. Additional charging points delivered during FY2026?\n27 will be onboarded after mobilisation under the applicable ongoing work streams \nand charging-point pricing arrangements.  \nAcceptance of an existing charging point will require testing of all applicable \nfunctionality supported by that charging point. The detailed acceptance criteria, \nacceptance process and treatment of any charging point that does not satisfy the \nacceptance criteria will be negotiated with the successful tenderer. \nQuestion 7: What cyber security assessment is required at the tender stage? \nAnswer 7: At the tender stage, Defence?s cyber security assessment will focus on the \nSOC 2 and ISO requirements identified in the RFT. Detailed alignment with other \napplicable Defence requirements, will be undertaken with the successful tenderer \nduring mobilisation. \nQuestion 8: To which components does the 99.5% availability requirement apply? \nAnswer 8: The 99.5% availability requirement applies to the CMS platform. It does \nnot apply to the availability of individual charging points. \nQuestion 9: What must the pilot implementation demonstrate, and may capability be \nintroduced progressively during Work stream 1? \n\nAnswer 9: The pilot is intended to validate the proposed connection approach and \nkey CMS functionality. Capability may be introduced progressively during Work \nstream 1 \nQuestion 10: How does Defence envisage users accessing the digital interactive map \nrequired by Specification requirement 18? \nAnswer 10: Defence is open to tenderers proposing an appropriate delivery approach. \nThe map must be accessible through the Defence intranet and must, at a minimum, \nidentify charging points by location and display their operational status, including any \nunavailability due to maintenance. \nQuestion 11: How must site-level controllers or gateways connect to the CMS? \nAnswer 11: Any site level controller, gateway or other device used to support \nintegration with the CMS must connect exclusively through the dedicated EV \ncharging WAN using available ports on the local router or switch. It must not be \nphysically connected to, exchange data with, or provide any communications path to \nthe Defence Engineering Services Network (DESN), including any DESN-connected \nrouter, switch, meter or other device. The EV charging WAN is expected to \naccommodate this traffic in most cases. \nTenderers must not install an independent cellular connection. If additional \nconnectivity is required, the successful tenderer may propose additional Cradlepoint \ndevices using the same connectivity solution as the dedicated EV charging WAN. \nDefence approval must be obtained for any proposed integration hardware. \nQuestion 12: What site-level technical documentation will Defence provide to \nsupport connection design and load-management configuration? \nAnswer 12: Sites with identified capacity constraints have existing local load \nmanagement. During mobilisation, Defence will work with the successful tenderer to \nidentify further load-management and optimisation opportunities and will make \nrelevant site-level technical documentation available to support those activities and \nassociated testing. \nQuestion 13: How many physical charging units comprise the approximately 40 \ncharging points expected at contract commencement? \nAnswer 13: The current estate comprises one dual-socket charging unit, with all \nremaining charging units having a single connector. Defence expects this to remain \nunchanged at contract commencement. Up to three additional dual-socket charging \nunits are expected to be delivered during the first year of the contract.  \nQuestion 14: Are charger-reported OCPP meter values sufficient for recovering non-\nfleet charging costs? \nAnswer 14: Yes. Charger-reported OCPP meter values are sufficient for cost-\nrecovery purposes. Revenue-grade metering is not required. \n\nQuestion 15: Which assets will be subject to the Contractor Managed \nCommonwealth Assets requirements? \nAnswer 15: The requirements will apply only to Commonwealth assets physically \nheld by the successful tenderer. \nQuestion 16: How should payment-processing fees be treated, and who is responsible \nfor refunds, chargebacks and disputed transactions for non-fleet charging? \nAnswer 16: Payment gateway, merchant and transaction-processing fees are to be \nseparately identified during billing and treated as reimbursable pass-through costs. \nThe successful tenderer, as the CMS provider, will be responsible for managing \nrefunds, chargebacks and disputed transactions associated with non-fleet charging. \nQuestion 17: When does the annual fee for a charging point commence? \nAnswer 17: The annual fee for a charging point commences when Defence accepts its \nconnection to the CMS. The treatment of fees during any temporary unavailability \nwill be agreed with the successful tenderer. \nQuestion 18: How do the equipment and workmanship warranty requirements apply \nto a cloud-hosted CMS?  \nAnswer 18: The warranty requirements apply only to additional controllers or other \nhardware purchased and installed under the CMS contract. They do not apply to the \ncloud-hosted CMS itself. \nQuestion 19: Does Defence require automated smart scheduling of fleet-vehicle \ncharging from contract commencement? \nAnswer 19: No. Automated smart scheduling is a lower-priority capability and is not \nrequired from contract commencement. A roadmap describing how the solution could \nsupport smart scheduling of fleet-vehicle charging in the future is desirable. \nQuestion 20: Is the successful tenderer responsible for end-to-end incident \nmanagement? \nAnswer 20: No. The successful tenderer must detect and troubleshoot faults and \nnotify Defence. Where physical remediation is required, Defence or its nominated \ninfrastructure and maintenance contractor will undertake that remediation. The \nsuccessful tenderer must then assist to confirm that the fault has been rectified and \nrecord the relevant details. \nQuestion 21: Must the CMS provider maintain a charger compatibility register and \nundertake OCPP conformance and regression testing? \nAnswer 21: This is not a mandatory requirement. However, tenderers may propose a \ncharger compatibility register and OCPP conformance and regression-testing \ncapability as part of their solution. \n\nQuestion 22: Is forecasting future charging infrastructure and electrical-capacity \nrequirements a mandatory Work stream 3 service? \nAnswer 22: No. This is not a mandatory requirement. However, tenderers may \npropose advisory services that use available utilisation, demand, capacity and \nperformance data to support Defence?s planning for future charging infrastructure and \nelectrical capacity. \n------------END OF ADDENDUM NO. 6 ------------- \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026.txt', 'extracted_text': "================================================================================\nAUSTENDER DETAILS: Current ATM View - DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026\n================================================================================\n\nDetail URL: https://www.tenders.gov.au/Atm/Show/d356a65c-0769-4ca8-9a5c-e0bba6ca1417\nScraped At: 2026-09-16 15:01:50\n\nSTRUCTURED ATTRIBUTES:\n----------------------------------------\nATM ID: DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026\nAgency: Department of Defence - DSRG\nCategory: 81162000 - Software as a Service (SaaS - Cloud)\nClose Date & Time: 18-Sep-2026 12:00 pm (ACT Local Time) Show close time for other time zones\nPublish Date: 31-Jul-2026\nLocation: ACT, NSW, VIC, SA, WA, QLD, NT, TAS\nATM Type: Request for Tender\nAPP Reference: DCET-EVCI-CMS\nMulti Agency Access: No\nPanel Arrangement: No\nMulti-stage: No\nDescription: This procurement seeks to establish a CMS capability to support the monitoring, control and management of EV charging across the Defence estate. The solution is to enable secure charger connectivity, user access management, billing and cost recovery, and integration with Defence systems and third party platforms. The overarching objective is to deliver a scalable, secure and interoperable CMS that supports Defence?s transition to electric vehicles through the effective operational management of charging infrastructure.\nOther Instructions: \nConditions for Participation: Clause 2.4 Preparation and Transmission of Classified Tenders; and Clause 2.5 Defence Security Requirements.\nTimeframe for Delivery: 1 year with option for 5x1 year extensions\nAddress for Lodgement: Tender responses are to be submitted electronically via the AusTender website www.tenders.gov.au using the 'Lodgement Page' link on this tender notification.\nAddenda Available: View Addenda\n\n================================================================================\n"}, {'file_name': 'DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026_Covering Letter.txt', 'extracted_text': 'OFFICIAL \nASDEFCON (Services) \n \n \nCovering Letter (V4.1) \n1 \nOFFICIAL \n \n \nSecurity and Estate Group \nInfrastructure Division \n26 Brindabella Circuit, Canberra Airport ACT 2609 \nMrs \nAmy \nBarker, \nAssistant \nDirector \nDCET,  \nE: ev.charging@defence.gov.au, T: 02 5109 8648 \n \nYou are invited to submit a tender in response to RFT DCET-EVCI-CMS-2026 to provide EV Charging \nManagement System (CMS) supply, integration and support services. \nThis procurement seeks to establish a CMS capability to support the monitoring, control and \nmanagement of EV charging across the Defence estate. The solution is to enable secure charger \nconnectivity, user access management, billing and cost recovery, and integration with Defence systems \nand third party platforms. The overarching objective is to deliver a scalable, secure and interoperable \nCMS that supports Defence?s transition to electric vehicles through the effective operational \nmanagement of charging infrastructure.  \nThis RFT has been prepared utilising the ASDEFCON (Services) template, with material amendments \nto the template identified in the Matrix of Changes at Attachment A to this letter.  While all care is taken \nby the Commonwealth in preparing Attachment A, it is a summation and therefore may not be complete \nand accurate.  You should not exclusively rely on Attachment A to identify template changes in this RFT \nand should exercise independent skill and judgement in reading and understanding this RFT and in \npreparing your tender. \nTenders are to be submitted by the Closing Time specified in the Tender Details Schedule.  Any \nquestions in relation to this RFT should be directed to the Contact Officer specified in the Tender Details \nSchedule. \nSubject to the conditions of tender, the proposed schedule for evaluation and negotiation is: \n? \nTenderers to submit responses by 28/08/2026 \n? \nResponses evaluated by 18/09/2026 \n? \nNegotiation finalised and contract awarded by 16/10/2026. \nTenderers should note that the above schedule is subject to change at any time and is not to be relied \non by tenderers as final and definitive. \n \nYours sincerely \nAmy Barker \n30/07/2026 \n \nAttachments: \nA. Matrix of Changes \n \n\nOFFICIAL \nASDEFCON (Services) \n \nATTACHMENT A \nCovering Letter (V4.1) \n2 \nOFFICIAL \n \nMATRIX OF CHANGES \nPart and Title \nAmended/New \nClause No. and \nchange type \n(Add/Modify/Delete) \nOther clauses \naffected by the \namendment \nDetails of Change \nPart 1: Conditions \nof Tender \n3.1.1 - Modify \nN/A \nModified Evaluation Criteria and \nProcess descriptions to better align \nwith requirements of this \nprocurement. \nPart 1: Conditions \nof Tender \nAnnex C: \nInformation to be \nprovided by the \ntenderer \n1.1a - Modify \nN/A \nModified description of information \nto be included in submission to \nexplicitly address mitigation of risks \nidentified in SOW. \nPart 2: Conditions \nof Contract \nItem 8 - New \n1.4 \nAdded ?Term of Contract? item to \nreflect desired contract length and \nextension flexibility. \nPart 2: Conditions \nof Contract \n7.1.8 \n7.1.10 \nAdded cyber liability insurance \nrequirement. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n'}], 'content_hash': None, 'tags': None, 'first_seen_at': None, 'last_scanned_at': '2026-09-16T15:02:53.969715+00:00', 'updated_at': None, 'raw_extra': None}
