2026-09-16T15:04:06.508867Z [] {'tender_id': None, 'source_reference_id': None, 'source_id': None, 'title': None, 'issuing_agency': None, 'category': None, 'status': None, 'publish_date': None, 'closing_date': None, 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': None, 'value_notes': None, 'location': None, 'description': '', 'contact_name': None, 'contact_email': None, 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': None, 'documents': [{'file_name': '26AMSA059 - Addendum No 3 -  AtoN Technical Support Services.txt', 'extracted_text': " \nProcurement \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \n \n \nREQUEST FOR TENDER 26AMSA059 - ADDENDUM NUMBER 3 \nATON TECHNICAL SUPPORT SERVICES \nThe following information is provided to assist tenderers in responding to RFT 26AMSA059, which \ncloses at, 2:00 PM (Canberra local time) 22 September 2026. \n \nNote to Respondents No. 1 ? Schedule 6 Tenderer?s Response Template  \nSchedule 6 Tenderer?s Response Template provides guidance as to the evaluation criteria and the \nquestions that relate to each criterion.  \nTenderers are advised that consistent with clause 10.3.1 of the issued RFT AMSA may use any \nresponses provided to inform the evaluation of any criterion.  \n \nResponse to Question 2 a) of Addendum Number 2: \nThe Service Management System must be hosted onshore within Australia including all data, \nbackups, logs etc. \nThe Service Management System should be hosted by the Contractor either under a cloud, on \npremises or SaaS platform. \nAll data and information provided or entered by AMSA in the Service Management System remains \nthe property of AMSA at all times and must be returned or destroyed at AMSAs direction. \nThe Service Management System must conform to, and be managed in accordance with: \n? \nthe Australian Government Information Security Manual or other recognised security standard \nsuch as ISO27001. \n? \nthe Australian Signals Directorate ?Essential 8 Strategies to Mitigate Cyber Incidents. \nThe Contractor must provide reasonable access to resources to allow AMSA to undertake security \nassessments, audits, reviews, penetration tests and Information Security Registered Assessor \nProgram (IRAP) assessments. \nThe Contractor is responsible for ensuring that the security of the Service Management System is \nmaintained and updated to prevent unauthorised access or modification to the System. \nThe Contractor must not use AMSA Information for the training, testing or operation of artificial \nintelligence or machine learning models unless approved in writing by AMSA. \nAdditional information in response to Question 2 f) of Addendum Number 2: \nAll user interfaces should be compliant with, or equivalent to, Web Content Accessibility Guidelines \n(WCAG) 2.0 at a minimum A level. \n \nQuestion 1: \nRef: Schedule 2 (Statement of Requirements) cl. 9.7.1 (configuration and testing of Assets and Spare \nParts on request); cl. 9.7.3 (root-cause investigation of all Asset failures in the Contractor's \npossession). \na) Can AMSA provide indicative annual volumes, or historical figures over a recent period (for \nexample the last two Contract Years), for (a) Asset and Spare Part configuration and testing \nunder cl. 9.7.1, and (b) Asset failure root-cause investigations under cl. 9.7.3?  \n\n \nAddendum 3 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \nb) Can AMSA confirm whether the obligations in cl. 9.7.1 and cl. 9.7.3 are capped or otherwise \nbounded, or whether they are wholly carried within the fixed Technical Support Services Fee \nregardless of volume?  \nc) If uncapped, can AMSA confirm the mechanism (if any) by which additional or abnormal \nconfiguration, testing or failure-investigation workload beyond an expected baseline is \nrecognised or reimbursed?  \nAnswer 1: \na) AMSA does not have statistics for all requested information however advises: \n? \nFault and Failure data was provided in Addendum 2 \n? \nIn the 25/26 FY the Technical Support Contractor completed 273 \nInspections/configurations of Assets which includes any Failure root cause \ninvestigations \nb) The requirements are included in the fixed Technical Support Services Fee. \nc) Clause 9.13 clarifies where Asset and AtoN Equipment related works may be reimbursed by \nAdditional Services. \n \nQuestion 2: \nRef: Schedule 2 (SoR) cl. 9.10.1 (Electrical Design Standard referenced); cl. 9.10.3 (calculations \nrequiring the Standard); 26AMSA059 Tender Information Package. \na) Can AMSA provide the AMSA Aid to Navigation Electrical Design Standard referenced at cl. \n9.10.1 and required for the calculations at cl. 9.10.3?  \nb) Alternatively, can AMSA confirm the design basis tenderers should assume for the cl. 9.10 \ncalculations, so that pricing and methodology can be prepared consistently across all \ntenderers?  \n \nAnswer 2: \nTenderers are to note that AMSA has not utilised this provision with the term of the current Technical \nSupport Contract, it is included primarily to ensure that AMSA maintains this capability should its \ninternal resources not be able to fulfil this requirement in the future.  \nThis requirement is considered a low risk/priority and as such Tenderers should advise (in Schedule 4 \nStatement of Non-Compliance) if they do not currently have and do not intend to develop the \ncapability to provide this requirement. Should the successful Tenderer not propose to provide this \nrequirement it may be addressed during contract negotiations. \na) The AMSA Aid to Navigation Electrical Design Standard is attached to this Addendum.  \nb) The Aid to Navigation Electrical Design Standard should be utilised however Tenderers \nshould note Clause 9.10.3 states ?AMSA will pay the Contractor?s reasonable costs in \naccordance with Schedule 4 of the Conditions of Contract for the provision of AtoN Light and \npower supply designs.?. \n \nIssued on 04 September 2026, and authorised by:  \n \nChris Evans  \nADVISOR PROCUREMENT \nFINANCE AND BUSINESS SERVICES \nCORPORATE SERVICES \n\n"}, {'file_name': '26AMSA059 - AtoN Technical Support Services - Addendum 1.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \nProcurement \nAddendum 1 ? 26AMSA059 ? Provision of Regional Aids to Navigation (AtoN) Maintenance Services \nPage 1 of 1 \n \nREQUEST FOR TENDER 26AMSA059 ? ADDENDUM NUMBER 1 \nFor the Provision of Regional Aids to Navigation (AtoN) \nMaintenance Services \nThe following Questions and Answers are provided to assist tenderers in responding to RFT \n26AMSA059, which closes at 2:00PM, (Canberra local time) 22 September 2026 \n \n \nA note to respondents:  \n  \nCorrection to Issue Date \nRespondents are advised that the issue date stated in the ATM was incorrect. \nThe issue date was published as 7 August 2026. The correct issue date is 10 August 2026. \nThis addendum is issued to correct the administrative error. All other information contained in the \nATM remains unchanged. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nIssued on 11th August 2026, and authorised by:  \n \nTabitha Gordon \nProcurement Officer | Procurement  \nGovernance | Corporate Services  \nAustralian Maritime Safety Authority \n18 Marcus Clarke St, Canberra, ACT, 2601  \nGPO Box 2181, Canberra ACT 2601  \n+61 2 6279 5008  \nTabitha.gordon@amsa.gov.au | www.amsa.gov.au \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': '26AMSA059 - AtoN Technical Support Services - Addendum No 2.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \nProcurement \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \n \n \nREQUEST FOR TENDER 26AMSA059 - ADDENDUM NUMBER 2 \nATON TECHNICAL SUPPORT SERVICES \nThe following information is provided to assist tenderers in responding to RFT 26AMSA059, which \ncloses at, 2:00 PM (Canberra local time) 22 September 2026. \n \nNote to respondents No 1: Correction to Approach to Market Title \nAMSA advises that a typographical error was identified in the title of the Approach to Market (ATM) \ndocumentation. The title should read: \nAids to Navigation (AtoN) Technical Support Services \nReferences to Regional AtoN Maintenance Services" within the ATM documents should be taken to \nmean "AtoN Technical Support Services". \nThis correction is administrative in nature only and does not alter the scope, requirements, conditions \nor intent of the ATM. \n \nNote to respondents No 2: Information Package for 26AMSA059 \nAMSA advises that the Information Package available for 26AMSA059 ? Aids to Navigation Essential \nMaintenance Services differs from the Information Package provided for 26AMSA060. \nThe 26AMSA059 Information Package contains additional documentation and information specific to \nthe operation and delivery of services under Contract 26AMSA059, including: \n? \nRemote Monitoring System manuals; \n? \nStratum contact information; \n? \nAtoN Inventory Stock Lists; and \n? \nJob Plans. \nTenderers should not assume that access to the 26AMSA060 Information Package provides access \nto the information relevant to 26AMSA059. \nTenderers wishing to obtain the 26AMSA059 Information Package must submit a separate request to \nAMSA in accordance with the Approach to Market requirements.  \n \nNote to respondents No 3: WHS accreditation not applicable to Technical Support Services \nAMSA advises that the requirement for accreditation under the Work Health and Safety (WHS) \nAccreditation Scheme, as outlined in Clause 2.8 of the Approach to Market (ATM), does not apply to \nthe AtoN Technical Support Services Contract. \nThe services to be provided under this Contract are not classified as building work for the purposes of \nthe WHS Accreditation Scheme. Accordingly, contractors are not required to hold WHS Accreditation \nto be eligible to provide services under this Contract. \nAll other applicable work health and safety obligations and requirements specified in the Contract and \nStatement of Requirements remain unchanged and must be complied with by the contractor. \n \n \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \nNote to respondents No 4: Contractor Interface Guide  \nProvided in this addendum is the AMSA Contractor Interface Guide for contract 26AMSA059 and \n26AMSA060.  \nThis Interface Guide outlines the operational interfaces and interactions, responsibilities, \ncoordination requirements and communication protocols between the Technical Support Contractor \nunder 26AMSA059 and the Regional Maintenance Contractors under 26AMSA060.  \n \nQuestion 1:  \nWe would like to ask whether AMSA intends to hold a tender briefing, industry information session or \nquestion and answer session for this ATM. If a session is planned, we would appreciate the date and \njoining details. If none is currently planned, we would welcome the opportunity to attend one should \nAMSA decide to schedule it. \nAnswer 1: \nAMSA confirms that no industry briefing, tender briefing, information session or question and answer \nsession is proposed for this Approach to Market (ATM). Tenderers should submit any requests for \nclarification in accordance with the ATM. \n \nQuestion 2: \nSchedule 2 cl. 3.8 requires the Contractor to provide, implement and operate a web-based Service \nManagement System (SMS) to log, track and communicate work tasks and requests between the \nContractor, AMSA, the AtoN Service Providers and other nominated parties. The SMS must provide \ninventory, freight, request, Additional Services and Fault and Failure functions (cl. 3.8.2), real-time \ndashboards, configurable workflows, data and attachment extraction (cl. 3.8.4), federated \nauthentication and mandatory multi-factor authentication (cl. 3.8.7).  \n \nUnder cl. 3.8.8(a) the SMS is to manage work tasks and requests that are not managed via the AMSA \nWork Management Application. To scope and price the deliverable, can AMSA please confirm the \nfollowing:  \n \na) Does AMSA require the SMS to be hosted onshore in Australia, and is a cloud/SaaS \nplatform acceptable, or is on-premise or sovereign hosting required? Please confirm \nany mandatory security requirements applicable to the storage of AMSA data (for \nexample IRAP assessment, ASD Essential Eight maturity level, ISO 27001 or \nProtective Security Policy Framework obligations).  \nb) Approximately how many users of each type (AMSA staff, AtoN Service Providers \nand other third parties) does AMSA anticipate, and does AMSA expect these \nexternal parties to have full functional (licensed) access or request/portal-only \naccess?  \nc) Please confirm AMSA\'s identity provider for the federated authentication requirement \nat cl. 3.8.7 (for example Microsoft Entra ID) and the acceptable protocols (for \nexample SAML 2.0 or OIDC).  \nd) Can AMSA confirm the functional boundary between the SMS and the AMSA Work \nManagement Application under cl. 3.8.8(a), and whether any integration or interface \nbetween the two systems is required?  \ne) For the extraction requirement at cl. 3.8.4, does AMSA require API-based \nintegration, or are scheduled exports in a defined format acceptable; and is \nintegration with any AMSA system (for example the Asset Information Management \nSystem (Maximo) or the Metocean Data Gateway) required?  \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \nf) \nWill AMSA accept an established commercial (COTS) IT service management \nplatform configured to meet the requirements rather than a purpose-built system, and \nare there any mandatory platform features, standards or accessibility requirements \n(for example WCAG 2.1) the SMS must meet? RFI-002 ? Schedule \n \nAnswer 2: \na) AMSA advises that Question 2(a) will be addressed separately in a forthcoming addendum. \nb) AMSA Staff ? 5 full functional licensed access \nAtoN Service Providers and other parties ? 15 users with request portal only \nTenderers should advise any additional cost for each extra type of user if applicable. \nc) AMSA uses Microsoft Entra ID, further details will be provided during Transition \nd) AMSA does not specifically require an interface between the SMS and AMSA Work \nManagement Application, however Tenderers should advise in their tender response: \nI. \nwhether an interface is available/achievable  \nII. \nif so what is proposed \nIII. \nany additional cost to implement \ne) API based integration is not required, scheduled exports in a defined format agreed with \nAMSA are acceptable. Integration with AMSA systems is not a specified requirement, \nhowever if opportunities are identified AMSA may engage the Contractor via Additional \nServices to undertake the integration at a later date. \nf) \nTenderers are required to propose a SMS that meets the requirements as set out in Schedule \n2 cl. 3.8. AMSA has not established a detailed set of mandatory requirements beyond those \nstated in the Tender documentation, Tenderers should advise the features, standards or \naccessibility requirements that the proposed SMS offers within the Tender response. \n \nTenderers may propose other functions that their Service Management System offers and provide \noptional pricing for AMSAs consideration and/or future implementation. \n \nQuestion 3: \nATM cl. 10.2.1 sets the qualitative evaluation criteria and weightings: Capability 30%, Experience \n20%, Methodology 25%, Availability & Timing 15% and Compliance 10%, with Practical Scenarios \nrecorded as a Value for Money assessment that is \'not weighted\'. Schedule 6 is the Tenderer\'s \nResponse Template against which the technical response is prepared and scored.  \n \nThe Schedule 6 template sets the questions to be answered and any applicable word limits. Please \nconfirm the number of questions, the word limits and how the response maps to the evaluation \ncriteria.  \n \na) to be answered in Schedule 6, and confirm the word (or page) limit applicable to each \nquestion?  \nb) Can AMSA confirm that the evaluation weightings at ATM cl. 10.2.1 (Capability 30%, \nExperience 20%, Methodology 25%, Availability & Timing 15%, Compliance 10%) govern the \nassessment, and confirm how each Schedule 6 question maps to those criteria?  \nc) Can AMSA clarify the treatment of the Practical Scenarios: cl. 10.2.1 lists Practical Scenarios \nas a Value for Money item that is \'not weighted\', while the Methodology criterion (25%) states \nits assessment will consider the practical-scenario response. Please confirm whether the \npractical scenarios are scored within the 25% Methodology weighting, assessed separately \nas an unweighted Value for Money input, or both.  \n \nAnswer 3: \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \na) The total number of questions to be answered in Schedule 6, and the word limit applicable to \neach question are set out in the Table included on page 1 of Schedule 6 ? Tenderers \nResponse to Schedule 2. \nb) Confirmed, the Table included on page 1 of Schedule 6 ? Tenderers Response to Schedule 2 \noutlines how each question maps to those criteria.  \nc) AMSA advises that the following words shall be deleted from the Methodology criterion shown \nin cl. 10.2.1: \n?The assessment of methodology will consider the extent to which the response to practical \nscenarios is assessed as meeting AMSAs requirements in relation to managing responses to \nFault and Failure and planning and executing technical support services?. \n \nFor the Avoidance of doubt, the responses to Questions 19 and 20 (Practical scenarios) will \nonly be considered in the value for money assessment and will not inform the assessment of \nthe weighted Qualitative Evaluation Criteria.  \n \nQuestion 4: \nSchedule 2 cl. 4.1.1 requires the Contractor to complete all Transition Services by the \nCommencement Date, and cl. 4.2.1 requires the Transition Plan to be submitted for approval by 1 \nMarch 2027, ahead of Commencement on 1 July 2027. Schedule 4 cl. 2.4.2 governs the Transition \nServices Fee.  \nThe Transition Period therefore spans approximately four months of Contractor effort ? recruitment, \nsystems establishment, inventory, facilities and readiness activities ? prior to the Commencement \nDate.  \n \na) Can AMSA confirm the payment mechanism for the Transition Services Fee under Schedule \n4 cl. 2.4.2 ? specifically, whether entitlement to payment accrues progressively against \nmilestones in the approved Transition Plan, or only on or after the Commencement Date?  \nb) If entitlement accrues progressively, can AMSA confirm the milestones against which \npayment claims may be made, and the invoicing and payment terms that apply to each?  \n \nAnswer 4: \nSchedule 4 cl 2.4 of the draft Contract (Schedule 1 of ATM) contains the information requested on \nwhen the Transition Fee is payable. \n \nQuestion 5:  \nATM cl. 2.5(a)?(b) records that responsibility for the Metocean System has been split: the Technical \nSupport Service Provider (TSP) is responsible for remote monitoring and management, and the \nRegion 1 Contractor is responsible for field maintenance. The Region 1 Contractor coordinates the \njoint annual Hammond Hill (AN551) visit and provides site transport for TSP personnel.  \nSchedule 2 cl. 9.4.8?9.4.10 require the TSP to conduct one annual Essential Maintenance Site Visit \nto AN551 Hammond Hill, coordinated with and led by the AtoN Service Provider. Clause 9.4.7(m) \nmakes the TSP responsible for reconfiguring wave rider buoy receivers during buoy changeouts by \nthe AtoN Service Provider, and cl. 9.4.11 confirms the TSP is not responsible for on-site Fault or \nFailure response to Metocean Equipment, maintenance of the wave rider buoys, or the Under Keel \nClearance Management System.  \n \na) Can AMSA confirm the demarcation of Metocean field tasks between the TSP\'s annual \nEssential Maintenance Site Visit to AN551 Hammond Hill (cl. 9.4.8) and the Region 1 \nContractor\'s field maintenance responsibilities, so the two scopes are complete and non-\noverlapping?  \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \nb) For wave rider buoy changeouts, can AMSA confirm the interface under cl. 9.4.7(m) ? that \nthe AtoN Service Provider performs the physical changeout and the TSP reconfigures the \nreceivers remotely ? and confirm the TSP is not responsible for buoy maintenance under cl. \n9.4.11(b)?  \nc) Can AMSA confirm the joint-attendance arrangements at Hammond Hill (cl. 9.4.9?9.4.10), \nincluding that the AtoN Service Provider leads timing, site access, security and safety, and \nprovides transport for TSP personnel?  \n \nAnswer 5: \na) In relation to infield maintenance conducted at AtoN Sites, the Contractor is only required to \nundertake maintenance at AN551 Hammond Hill AtoN Site, which is to be conducted annually \nin conjunction with the AtoN Service Providers planned AtoN Site Visit. During the infield \nmaintenance the Contractor is required to undertake the requirements detailed in Section 5 of \nthe Metocean System Service- Infield Job Plan. \nFor the avoidance of doubt the Contractors maintenance responsibilities relate only to \nequipment installed internal to the building and specifically the requirements as stated in the \nJob Plan. The Contractor is not responsible for installation and/or replacement of equipment. \nTenderers should also refer to the following for clarification of responsibilities for the \nMetocean System: \n? \nThe AtoN Service Provider Job Plans that are provided for information and are \nincluded in the Information Package \n? \nThe AMSA Contractor Interface Guide for Contracts 26AMSA059 and \n26AMSA060 \nb) Confirmed \nc) Confirmed \n \nQuestion 6: \nSchedule 2 cl. 9.1.4 requires the Contractor to have suitably trained technical support staff available \nto provide comprehensive Technical Support twelve (12) hours per day, seven (7) days per week, \nbetween 7am and 7pm AEST. Clause 9.1.5 sets response times within that period (30 minutes for \nmost requests, including remote assistance and fault-finding/outage requests, and 120 minutes for \nmatters such as Asset configuration or commissioning).  \n \nClause 9.1.6 provides that outside those hours the Contractor must have available, upon request of \nAMSA, a single after-hours point of contact able to provide Technical Services support for the \ninvestigation, troubleshooting or restoration of AtoNs as part of Fault and Failure response.  \n \na) Can AMSA confirm that, outside the 7am?7pm AEST period in cl. 9.1.4, the obligation under \ncl. 9.1.6 is to provide a single after-hours point of contact (an on-call arrangement) rather than \na continuously staffed support desk?  \nb) Can AMSA confirm the response time(s) expected of the after-hours point of contact under cl. \n9.1.6 ? for example, the time to acknowledge and to commence remote investigation of a \nFault or Failure after AMSA makes a request?  \nc) Can AMSA confirm whether the after-hours point of contact is engaged only \'upon request of \nAMSA\' (as cl. 9.1.6 states), or whether the Contractor is also expected to self-initiate after-\nhours response to alarms detected through the 24/7 remote monitoring obligation in cl. 8.1?  \n \nAnswer 6: \na) Confirmed \nb) 60 minutes to commence action or respond to a request. \nc) It is expected that the Contractor will self-initiate the afterhours support where it is necessary \nfor the Contractor to undertake remote monitoring tasks in night time hours such as polling or \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \nrestarting lanterns and lighting equipment to investigate Fault and Failures and confirm \ncorrect operation of AtoN. \n \nClauses 9.1.5 and 9.1.6 are amended as per below: \n9.1.5 \nDuring the twelve (12) hours per day seven (7) days per week Technical Support period, the \nContractor must have the necessary systems and resources to commence action and \nrespond to requests for Technical Support and meet the following response times:  \na) Routine Technical Support ? 30 minutes or as otherwise agreed with the requesting party \nfor non-urgent matters,  \nb) Remote assistance whilst undertaking Essential Maintenance, Fault and Failure Response \nor other works at an AtoN Site ? 30 minutes, and \nc) Other matter such as Technical Support for configuration/commissioning of AtoN \nEquipment and/or freight of items from the Contractors Inventory ? 120 minutes or as \notherwise agreed with requesting party for non-urgent matters. \n \n9.1.6 \nOutside of the hours shown in clause 9.1.4 the Contractor must have available upon request \nof AMSA, a single after-hours point of contact able to provide Technical Support Services for \nthe investigation, troubleshooting or restoration of AtoNs as part of Fault and Failure \nResponse. The Contractor must be able to commence action and respond to requests within \n60 Minutes. The Contractor will self-initiate the afterhours support where it is necessary for \nthe Contractor to undertake remote monitoring tasks in nighttime hours such as polling or \nrestarting lanterns and lighting equipment to investigate Fault and Failures and confirm \ncorrect operation of AtoN. \n \nQuestion 7: \nSchedule 2 cl. 9.1.4 requires comprehensive Technical Support to be available twelve (12) hours per \nday, seven (7) days per week between 7am and 7pm AEST.                                                                   \n \nCan AMSA confirm whether the 7am?7pm \'AEST\' support window in cl. 9.1.4 is fixed at UTC+10 \nyear-round, or is intended to follow local eastern clock time (AEST/AEDT), such that the window \nobserves daylight saving?  \n \nAnswer 7: \nThe requirement is AEST as stated which does not observe daylight savings. \n \nQuestion 8: \nSchedule 2 cl. 3.8.10 requires the Fault and Failure function of the Service Management System to be \nestablished and operational prior to the Commencement Date. Clause 3.8.11 records AMSA\'s \npreference for all SMS functions to be operational prior to the Commencement Date, but provides \nthat, if that is not possible, all other functions must be established and operational prior to 30 \nSeptember 2027.  \na) Can AMSA confirm that the Fault and Failure function is the only Service Management \nSystem capability that must be operational at the Commencement Date under cl. 3.8.10, and \nthat all other functions may be phased to be operational by 30 September 2027 under cl. \n3.8.11?  \nb) Can AMSA confirm whether it has any preference, priority or expected sequence for delivery \nof the remaining (non-fault) functions between the Commencement Date and 30 September \n2027?  \n \nAnswer 8: \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \na) Confirmed \nb) AMSA has not identified a priority or expected sequence for delivery of the non-Fault and \nFailure functions, tenders should advise their  proposed sequence and timing in Response \nSchedule 6.  \n \nQuestion 9: \nSchedule 2 cl. 9.4.5 requires the Contractor to establish and maintain a Support and Maintenance \nContract with Stratum ICT for the software, application and control components they provide and \nmaintain, and cl. 9.4.6 requires the Contractor to establish and maintain remote access to AMSA \nsystems for Stratum ICT personnel. Clause 9.4.2 identifies numerous Metocean components \nattributed to Stratum ICT (for example Velo CT software, the Windows Server environment, SQL \nServer, Nuance speech product and SeaMAX). The Technical Support Information Package is stated \nto include contact details for Stratum ICT.  \n \na) Can AMSA confirm the contracting path for the arrangement required under cl. 9.4.5 ? \nspecifically, whether AMSA will novate or assign the Stratum ICT support and maintenance \ncontract it presently holds to the incoming Contractor at Commencement, or whether the \nContractor is required to establish a new contract with Stratum ICT, and confirm the date by \nwhich that step is to be completed?  \nb) Can AMSA confirm whether the cost of the Stratum ICT Support and Maintenance Contract is \nrecovered through the fixed Technical Support Services Fee or as a reimbursable cost under \nSchedule 4, and confirm the mechanism by which that recovery is adjusted if Stratum ICT\'s \ncharges change during the term?  \n \nAnswer 9: \na) AMSA does not currently hold a Stratum ICT support and maintenance contract. It is the \nresponsibility of the successful Contractor to establish a contract with Stratum ICT and to \nhave this arrangement in place at the Commencement Date.  \nb) The cost of the Stratum ICT Support and Maintenance Contract is recovered through the fixed \nTechnical Support Services Fee.  \n \nQuestion 10: \nATM cl. 2.5(e) records that AMSA will reimburse Contractors for approved Spare Parts, while \nContractors remain responsible for Consumables under the fixed Contract price, and that the \nConsumables definition has been updated. Schedule 2 cl. 1.2.1 gives \'Consumable\' and \'Spare Parts\' \nthe meanings given in the Contract.  \n \na) Can AMSA provide the current (updated) definition of \'Consumable\' referred to in ATM cl. \n2.5(e), together with the definition of \'Spare Parts\', as they appear in the Draft Contract?  \nb) Can AMSA provide worked examples at the Consumable / Spare Part boundary for AtoN \nelectronic components ? for example, which of items such as connectors, fuses, fixings, \ncable, modules, sub-assemblies and replaceable electronic components are treated as \nConsumables versus reimbursable Spare Parts?  \nc) Can AMSA confirm whether the classification is determined by item type, by unit value \nthreshold, by the Qualified Equipment List / Inventory Stock List, or by another mechanism?  \n \nAnswer 10: \na) The definitions are included in the draft Contract and Statement of Requirements. \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \nb) Examples of Consumable are included within the definition. Examples of Spare Parts are \nlisted in the Qualified Equipment List (QEL) ?Equipment Spare Parts? tab. The QEL is \navailable in the information package. \nc) The classification of Spare Part or Consumable is determined by the definition. \n \nQuestion 11: \nSchedule 2 cl. 9.12.1 requires the Contractor to attend a minimum of four (4) AtoN Site Visits with \nAtoN Service Providers each Contract Year for knowledge and awareness, involving a variety of \nContractor Personnel. Clause 9.12.5 requires allowance for two (2) personnel to participate in a \nminimum of two (2) AtoN Site Visits (four separate site visits in total), with an option to combine into a \nsingle mobilisation by one person by agreement with AMSA. Clause 9.12.4 requires the Contractor to \nprovide its own accommodation and transport to the point of mobilisation.  \nThe AtoN Service Provider makes allowance in non-commercial transport (vessels, helicopters) for \nContractor personnel and inducts them into its safety systems (cl. 9.12.3).  \na) Can AMSA confirm that the Contractor\'s travel and accommodation costs for the four annual \nAtoN Site Visits (to the point of mobilisation, per cl. 9.12.4) are to be borne by the Contractor \nwithin the fixed fee and are non-reimbursable?  \nb) Can AMSA provide guidance on the expected geographic spread of the four annual site visits \n? for example, whether visits are expected to be distributed across remote and non-remote \nRegions each year, and any indicative mobilisation points ? so the associated travel cost can \nbe estimated on a common basis?  \n \nAnswer 11: \na) Confirmed \nb) The actual AtoN Sites will be selected in consultation between AMSA, the Contractor and the \nRegional AtoN Service Providers. It is anticipated that the AtoN Sites to be visited will be \naccessible from regional commercial airports and will require 1 day per AtoN Site visit plus \ntravel time to and from the location. The actual sites visited will be dependent on the \nContractors appetite to visit remote locations and gain an understanding of the work being \nconducted by the AtoN Service Providers, the unique and challenging locations, structures \nand AtoN Systems in the AMSA AtoN Network. \n \nNote: If a Tenderer is successful in being awarded a Contract under tender 26AMSA059 and also a \ncontract for a region under tender 26AMSA060 AMSA may seek to remove this requirement during \ncontract negotiations noting the benefit will be diminished. \n \nQuestion 12: \nThe Tenderer\'s Declaration is provided at Schedule 8 of the ATM and is a minimum content and \nformat requirement. The Tenderers\' Note on the Schedule 8 page states that lodgement of a \ncompleted Tenderer\'s Declaration substantially in the form set out in \'this Schedule 9\' is a minimum \ncontent and format requirement.  \n \nCan AMSA confirm that the reference to \'Schedule 9\' in the Tenderers\' Note on the Schedule 8 page \nis a typographical error and should read \'Schedule 8\', and that completing and lodging the Schedule 8 \nTenderer\'s Declaration satisfies the minimum content and format requirement?  \n \nAnswer 12: \nAMSA advises that the reference to ?Schedule 9? in the Tenderers\' Note is a typographical error and \nshould read ?Schedule 8?. \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \nTenderers must complete and submit the Tenderer\'s Declaration to satisfy the minimum content and \nformat requirements of the Approach to Market. \n \nQuestion 13: \nAttachment B, KPI Sub-Item 1.3 (Resource Readiness) requires that personnel, Inventory, specialist \ntools and freight services are maintained so that no Contractor-controlled resource deficiency impacts \ndelivery of the Contract Services. Section 4 defines the Critical Failure Event for KPI 1.3 as a \nContractor-controlled personnel, Inventory, specialist tool or freight service deficiency that delays or \nprevents delivery of Contract Services of this Contract, \'or any of the Regional AtoN Maintenance \nContracts\'. Under the Critical Failure Rule (Section 3), a Critical Failure Event results in a score of \nzero for that KPI Sub-Item for the assessment period.  \na) Can AMSA clarify how a resource deficiency that affects a separate Regional AtoN \nMaintenance Contract is attributed to the TSP under KPI 1.3 ? specifically, what must be \nshown to establish that the deficiency was \'Contractor-controlled\' on the part of the TSP \nrather than the regional contractor?  \nb) Can AMSA confirm the evidence standard and process it will apply before recording a KPI 1.3 \nCritical Failure Event that originates in delivery of a Regional AtoN Maintenance Contract, \nincluding any opportunity for the TSP to respond?  \nc) Will AMSA confirm that KPI 1.3 excludes deficiencies arising from the acts, omissions, \nscheduling or resource decisions of a regional contractor or of AMSA, and from approved \nconcessions or Unforeseen Events (consistent with Section 3 of Attachment B)?  \n \nAnswer 13: \na) The KPI considers where a deficiency in the Contractors provision of the Contract Services \nimpacts an AtoN Service Providers delivery of their contract services. An example may be \nwhen the Contractor fails to meet an agreed or specified timeframe that results in an impact to \nthe AtoN Service Providers planned/scheduled work.  \nb) The evidence to support a deduction or Critical Failure would vary depending on the specific \ndeficiency/failure. The Contractor is required to assess and report on their evaluation of their \nperformance against the KPIs Quarterly and the Quarterly Meeting includes review of the \nContractors performance against the KPIs. \nc) Confirmed. \n \nThe Scoring framework table included in section 2 of Attachment B Key Performance Indicators is \namended to below: \nRating \nScore \nDefinition \nFully achieved \n100% \nAll relevant success criteria achieved with no material deficiencies, impacts or non-\nconformances. \nMinor deficiencies \n75% \nMinor deficiencies identified, with no material impact on service delivery, data integrity, \ncompliance or AMSA operations. \nModerate deficiencies \n50% \nModerate deficiencies identified, that impact on service delivery, data integrity, compliance \nor AMSA operations. \nSignificant deficiencies \n25% \nSignificant or repeated deficiencies identified, or corrective action required to prevent or \nrectify service delivery, compliance or information management impacts. \nNot achieved / Critical \nfailure \n0% \nRequirement not achieved, critical failure event occurs, material service delivery impact \noccurs, or AMSA determines performance is unacceptable. \n \nQuestion 14: \nATM cl. 2.5(d) records that the Technical Support Service Provider is now responsible for triage and \ninitial investigation of all Faults and Failures, regardless of remote monitoring capability. Schedule 2 \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \ncl. 9.8.1 requires the TSP to provide remote support to AMSA and its AtoN Service Providers to \nrespond to AtoN Faults and Failures and restore services, and cl. 9.8.2 requires the TSP to respond \nto and investigate any alarm or notification as a potential Fault or Failure.  \n \nThe TSP\'s response is governed by AMSA\'s \'Managing Aids to Navigation Faults and Failures \nProcedure\' (cl. 9.8.3), which is stated to be included in the Technical Support Information Package.  \na) Can AMSA confirm that the TSP is the single first-line triage and initial-investigation point for \nall AtoN Faults and Failures across all Regions, including Faults and Failures at non-\nmonitored sites and those reported from sources other than remote monitoring (for example \nAtoN Service Providers, the RCC, mariners or third parties)?  \nb) Can AMSA confirm the intake channels through which Faults and Failures reach the TSP for \ntriage, and whether AtoN Service Providers and AMSA are required to route reports through \nthe TSP as the primary point?  \nc) Can AMSA confirm the interface between the TSP\'s triage/initial investigation and the \nregional contractors\' field response ? in particular, the point at which a Fault or Failure is \nreferred from the TSP to a regional contractor, and how that referral is recorded ? noting the \ndefinition of this boundary in the \'Managing Aids to Navigation Faults and Failures Procedure\'.  \n \nAnswer 14: \na) Confirmed. \nb) Intake channels for reports of Faults and Failures are varied and come from mariners, the \nRCC, members of the public, AMSA, other third parties etc. AMSA confirms that AMSA and \nthe AtoN Service Providers are required to route reports through the Contractor as the \nprimary point.  \nc) The point at which a Fault or Failure is referred from the Contractor to AtoN Service Provider \nis outlined in the \'Managing Aids to Navigation Faults and Failures Procedure\' provided in the \ninformation package, with the actual point or timing dependant on each Fault or Failure being \ninvestigated. Some specific time limits are included in the Managing Aids to Navigation Faults \nand Failures Procedure for in situations where the Contractor is unable to gain a confirming \nreport. The Contractor is required to utilise all means available to them to remotely investigate \nand rectify a Fault or Failure prior to referral to the AtoN Service Provider. The referral is to be \nrecorded in the AMSA Work Management Application, and in the Contractors Service \nManagement System.  \n \nQuestion 15: \nSchedule 2 cl. 11.2 requires the Contractor to obtain, maintain and use access to AMSA\'s Online \nPortal and AMSA Work Management Application, and identifies the Asset Information Management \nSystem (Maximo) as the system of record for Assets, Spare Parts, Fault and Failure records and \nmaintenance activities (cl. 11.2.5). Clause 11.4 sets out the use of the AMSA Work Management \nApplication for Work Orders, reports, Asset changes and site information, and cl. 3.8.4 requires data \nand attachments to be extractable from the Service Management System for use in other systems.  \nThe tender documents define the AMSA Work Management Application and the Asset Information \nManagement System (Maximo) but do not specify the technical method by which the Contractor\'s \nsystems (including the Service Management System) are to exchange data with them.  \na) Can AMSA confirm whether the required interaction with the AMSA Work Management \nApplication and the Asset Information Management System (Maximo) is interface-only \n(Contractor Personnel operating within AMSA\'s applications), or whether system-to-system / \nAPI integration between the Contractor\'s systems and AMSA\'s systems is required or \nexpected?  \nb) If system-to-system integration is required, can AMSA confirm the data exchange method and \nformat (for example REST API, scheduled file exchange, or other), the data sets in scope, \nand any licences, credentials or connectivity to be provided by AMSA versus the Contractor?  \n\n \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \nc) Can AMSA confirm the Contractor-side IT requirements and the transition-in activity expected \nto establish and test any integration prior to the Commencement Date?  \n \nAnswer 15: \na) AMSA can confirm interaction with the AMSA Work Management Application is interface only. \nThere are no specified requirements for system-to-system / API integration between the \nContractor\'s systems and AMSA\'s systems. However, the Contractor may propose API \nintegration where it is possible and provides efficiencies.  \nThe contractor is not required to directly interact AMSAs Asset Information Management \nSystem (Maximo). \nb) Not applicable \nc) Not applicable \n \nClause 3.1.4 shall be amended as per below to remove the reference to the Asset Information \nManagement system: \n \n3.1.4 \nIt is the Contractors responsibility to work with AMSA to establish access to, and ensure \npersonnel are trained in the use of, AMSA\'s Online Portal, AMSA Work Management \nApplication. \n \nQuestion 16: \nAttachment B sets the KPI framework for the new contract, and Schedule 2 cl. 12.3 and cl. 13.3 \nestablish the KPI and Fault and Failure reporting regime. The current (incumbent) Technical Support \narrangement will have generated performance data against comparable measures over its term.  \na) Will AMSA publish the incumbent Technical Support provider\'s KPI results for the first two \nyears of the current contract, at KPI Sub-Item level and by Region, including the number and \nnature of Critical Failure Events?  \nb) If Sub-Item / regional results are not available in that form, can AMSA release the underlying \nperformance data (for example Essential Maintenance completion rates, Fault and Failure \nresponse performance and volumes) sufficient to establish an operating baseline for the new \nKPI regime?  \n \nAnswer 16: \na) AMSA will not be providing any KPI results for the first two years of the current contract as \npart of this ATM process \nb) AMSA will not be providing any underlying performance data as part of this ATM process \n \nQuestion 17: \nAttachment A specifies the Essential Maintenance Activities, Site Service Intervals and Target Start \nDates for the AtoN Network. Schedule 2 cl. 11.1 records that AMSA holds a Site Asset Management \nStrategy (SAMS) for each AtoN Site containing site-specific information relevant to planning and \ndelivery, with access to the SAMS Portal provided prior to the Commencement Date. \'Corrective \nMaintenance\' (cl. 1.2.1) covers unplanned failures, defects or damage outside Essential Maintenance.  \na) Will AMSA provide an asset condition report (or equivalent condition baseline) for the AtoN \nNetwork by Region, sufficient to indicate the current condition of the Assets and AtoN \nEquipment the Contractor will support?  \nb) Alternatively, will AMSA release relevant SAMS extracts, and the recent defect and Fault and \nFailure history (for example the last two years), during the tender period, so tenderers can \nassess the likely Corrective Maintenance and Fault and Failure workload and required spares \nholdings?  \n\nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059\nProcurement \nProcurement \nAnswer 17: \na)\nAMSA will not be providing the asset condition report as part of this ATM process. The asset\ncondition report will be provided to the successful Contractor on contract commencement\nb)\nFault and Failure statistics for the previous two financial years are presented below\nFAULT AND FAILURE DATA \nThe below data documents the AtoN Fault and Failure Response Services conducted by the \nTechnical Support Contractor in the 24/25 and 25/26 Financial years it is provided for information only \nand is not an estimate of future AtoN Fault and Failure Response Services requirements. \nRegion \nTotal Faults and Failure \nrecorded by Technical Support \nContractor \nFaults remotely rectified by \nTechnical Support Contractor \nRegion 1 North Queensland \n(previously regions 1 & 2) \n336 \n207 \nRegion 3 South Queensland \n187 \n143 \nRegion 4 New South Wales \n64 \n42 \nRegion 5 Victoria and \nTasmania \n138 \n109 \nRegion 6 South Australia \n101 \n76 \nRegion 7 Western Australia \n162 \n104 \nRegion 8 Northern Territory \n24 \n12 \nTOTAL \n1012 \n693 \nIssued on 28 August 2026, and authorised by:\n \nChris Evans \nADVISOR PROCUREMENT \nFINANCE AND BUSINESS SERVICES \nCORPORATE SERVICES \nChris Evans\nDigitally signed by Chris Evans \nDate: 2026.08.28 14:36:19 \n+10\'00\'\n\n'}, {'file_name': '26AMSA059 - AtoN Technical Support Services - Addendum No 4.txt', 'extracted_text': " \nProcurement \nAddendum 2 ? 26AMSA059 \n \n \nAddendum 4 ? 26AMSA059 \nREQUEST FOR TENDER 26AMSA059 - ADDENDUM NUMBER 4 \nATON TECHNICAL SUPPORT SERVICES \nThe following information is provided to assist tenderers in responding to RFT 26AMSA059, which \ncloses at, 2:00 PM (Canberra local time) 22 September 2026. \n \nQuestion 1: \nWith regards to Schedule 2, Section 9.4.1 and 9.4.2: \na) Is the Contractor responsible for sourcing Windows Operating System and SQL Server \nlicences from Microsoft? \nb) If so, are these to be additional services or covered under the service fee? \nc) Section 9.4.1 e) refers to undertaking software upgrades, patching etc. as required or \nrequested. Section 9.4.2 b) refers to the Contractor being responsible for Microsoft \nWindows Server Operating System. Could AMSA clarify what may be required vs what \ncould be requested under these sections? \n \nAnswer 1: \na) No \nb) Not applicable \nc) There are currently no proactive software upgrades or patching of the system being \nundertaken. As per 9.4.1 e) AMSA may request or require specific software upgrades \nor patching be undertaken of the current system throughout the Term of the contract. \n \nQuestion 2: \nIn Addendum 2 - 26AMSA059, Answer 5 a), it is clear that the contractor is not responsible \nfor installation or replacement of equipment at AN551 Hammond Hill.  \na) Does AMSA intend on replacing server infrastructure in the MetOcean System during \nthe period of this contract? \nb) If so, is the contractor to provide Microsoft Windows Server Operating System and \nSQL Server licences and support? Is this to be additional services or covered under \nthe service fee? \n \nAnswer 2: \na) The existing servers have an indicative service life shorter than the Term of the \nproposed Contract, as such it is likely that the servers will be replaced within the \nContract Term. However, AMSA may separately tender and contract any such \nreplacement works. The Technical Support Contractor will remain responsible for the \nnew or upgraded servers in accordance with the Contract once they have been \ninstalled and commissioned. \nb) The Contractor must supply Microsoft Windows and SQL Server technical support \nwithin the fixed Technical Support Services Fee. AMSA own, and are responsible for, \nlicence fees. \n \n\n \nAddendum 4 ? 26AMSA059 \nProcurement \nProcurement \n \nQuestion 3: \nSchedule 2 (SoR) cl. 9.12.1 requires the Contractor to attend a minimum of four (4) AtoN \nSite Visits with AtoN Service Providers each Contract Year for knowledge and awareness \npurposes.  \nSeparately, cl. 9.4.8 and Addendum 2 A5(a) require the Contractor to undertake Essential \nMaintenance annually at AN551 Hammond Hill, in conjunction with the AtoN Service \nProvider?s planned AtoN Site Visit, limited to the tasks in Section 5 of the Metocean System \nService ? Infield Job Plan.  \na) Can AMSA confirm whether the annual AN551 Hammond Hill Essential Maintenance \nSite Visit under cl. 9.4.8 is counted as one of the minimum four AtoN Site Visits \nrequired under cl. 9.12.1, or is additional to. \n \nAnswer 3:  \nTenderers are advised that cl. 9.12.1 and cl 9.4.8 outline these as separate requirements, as \nsuch these requirements are independent of each other and the annual AN551 Hammond \nHill Essential Maintenance Site Visit under cl. 9.4.8 is not counted as one of the minimum \nfour AtoN Site Visits. \n \n \nIssued on 11 September 2026, and authorised by:  \n \n \n \nChris Evans  \nADVISOR PROCUREMENT \nFINANCE AND BUSINESS SERVICES \nCORPORATE SERVICES \nChris \nEvans\nDigitally signed by \nChris Evans \nDate: 2026.09.11 \n14:34:51 +10'00'\n\n"}, {'file_name': '26AMSA059 AtoN Technical Support Services ATM  - Final.txt', 'extracted_text': 'Procurement \nApproach to Market 26AMSA059 ? AtoN Technical Support Services  \nPage 1 of 32 \n \nApproach to Market \nATM 26AMSA059 \nFor the Provision of Regional Aids to Navigation (AtoN) Maintenance \nServices \n \n \nISSUE DATE: 7 AUGUST 2026 \nCLOSING TIME & DATE: 2:00pm, CANBERRA LOCAL TIME (AEST) 22 \nSEPTEMBER 2026 \nLODGEMENT ADDRESS: Via AusTender at https://www.tenders.gov.au \n \n\n \nApproach to Market 26AMSA059 ? AtoN Technical Support Services  \nPage 2 of 32 \nProcurement \nTable of contents \n1. \nDefinitions ................................................................................................... 4 \n2. \nIntroduction ................................................................................................. 6 \n2.1 Background to AMSA .............................