2026-09-16T15:04:50.212974Z [] {'tender_id': None, 'source_reference_id': None, 'source_id': None, 'title': None, 'issuing_agency': None, 'category': None, 'status': None, 'publish_date': None, 'closing_date': None, 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': None, 'value_notes': None, 'location': None, 'description': '', 'contact_name': None, 'contact_email': None, 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': None, 'documents': [{'file_name': '2007-2313 Hot Press Treatment Report Mar 03_2026.txt', 'extracted_text': 'Treatment Details\nIdentifier\n2007-2313 Hot Press\nLocation\nLF509 - Old Kitchen\nClient\nOld Parliament House\nAdditional Condition Notes\nThe hot press unit was found to contain friable asbestos within the door and wall\npanels, requiring controlled removal. My role was to carefully deconstruct the unit\nand remove accessible panels to allow the asbestos contractor clear access to the\nasbestos-containing components. Following initial panel removal, it became evident\nthat some panels could not be removed without significant intervention (including\ncutting water pipes and drilling out seized fixings). As the unit would remain an\nasbestos-containing object (due to the insulative mats under the top, which cannot\nbe removed), this level of damage was not pursued. Instead, the cracks and small\nholes present in the two southern wall panels required stabilisation.\nBefore Treatment Photos\nTreatment Report\nTREATMENT REPORT: Object or Fitting\nPage 1 of \nConservation Works Pty Ltd\n7\n\nHole in southern wall, west edge.\nTreatment Report\nTREATMENT REPORT: Object or Fitting\nPage 2 of \nConservation Works Pty Ltd\n7\n\nHoles in southern wall, eastern edge.\nAdditional Treatment Notes\nDoor and wall panels that could be removed without permanent alteration (e.g.\ncutting pipes or drilling out seized fixings) were deconstructed and removed to\nprovide the asbestos removalists improved access to the rear panels. Areas of\ncracked metal containment on the southern wall panels were stabilised by adhering\ngalvanised mild steel patches over the affected surfaces. Patches were fixed using a\nquick-setting epoxy resin, with edges sealed using a flexible black polyurethane\nsealant. Following completion of the asbestos works, the hot press was\nreconstructed using the original fixings in the original holes. Reconstruction was\nundertaken across two dates due to issues identified in the asbestos removalists?\nwork, requiring their return to site to improve flexible seals to the rear of the doors.\nDuring Treatment Photos\nDoor and wall panels removed, ready for asbestos removalists. - Asbestos was\naccessed by removing the sheet metal from the rear of the cast iron doors, the loose\nfill material is sandwiched between the cast iron and sheet metal.\nTreatment Report\nTREATMENT REPORT: Object or Fitting\nPage 3 of \nConservation Works Pty Ltd\n7\n\nRepair patches adhered in place.\nTreatment Report\nTREATMENT REPORT: Object or Fitting\nPage 4 of \nConservation Works Pty Ltd\n7\n\nRepair patch masked out ready for polyurethane sealant.\nAfter Treatment Photos\nTreatment Report\nTREATMENT REPORT: Object or Fitting\nPage 5 of \nConservation Works Pty Ltd\n7\n\nRecommendations\nFor all future works, including routine cleaning, handling and display, the hot press\nmust be managed as an asbestos-containing item. Friable and bound asbestos\nremains present, including within the integrated heat mats beneath the top (not\nremovable and accessible at the edges), and residual friable asbestos within the\nnorthern end wall panels, southern end wall panels, and the side wall panel at the\nsouth-west corner; these areas could not be removed due to being held in place with\npipes and seized fixings.\nThe unit should be inspected annually for any changes, with particular attention to\ncorrosion at the rear of door and wall panels which could expose asbestos.\nAny cleaning while on display should only be undertaken by personnel wearing full\nPPE, including a Tyvek suit, nitrile gloves, and a P3 asbestos half-face respirator.\nThe object would also benefit from general conservation works, including\nreplacement of missing fixings, detailed surface cleaning, and renewal of protective\ncoatings (e.g. protective wax to metal surfaces?previously applied in 2007). Minor\ndisruption to surface coatings occurred as a result of the asbestos works, and these\ncoatings should be renewed.\nTreatment Report\nTREATMENT REPORT: Object or Fitting\nPage 6 of \nConservation Works Pty Ltd\n7\n\nConservator\nGillian Mitchell\nDate/Time\n03-03-2026\nTreatment Report\nTREATMENT REPORT: Object or Fitting\nPage 7 of \nConservation Works Pty Ltd\n7\n\n'}, {'file_name': 'Attachment A - 1.0 MoAD01_260818-Document Transfer.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n \nMSAOM- DocTrans , Rev 3.0, 01/07/15 \nMoAD01_260818-Document Transfer \nDocument Register +Transmission \nProject \nMain Kitchen Exhibition \nProject N? \nMoAD01 \n \nMuseum of Australian Democracy \nRef N? \nMoAD01_MASTER-Document \nTransfer \n \nP l e a s e c o n t a c t u s i f a n y p a r t o f t h i s t r a n s m i s s i o n i s u n r e a d a b l e o r d o e s n o t c o n t a i n t h e s t a t e d n u m b e r o f d o c u m e n t s .  \nI M P O R T A N T N O T I C E T h e i n f o r m a t i o n c o n t a i n e d i n t h i s d o c u m e n t i s C O N F I D E N T I A L a n d m a y a l s o b e L E G A L L Y P R I V I L E G E D , i n t e n d e d o n l y f o r \nt h e a d d r e s s e e . I f y o u a r e n o t t h e a d d r e s s e e , a n y d i s s e m i n a t i o n a n d c o p y i n g o f t h i s d o c u m e n t s a r e s t r i c t l y p r o h i b i t e d . \n \n \n \n  \nPurpose \n \n \nDate of Issue \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nInformation \n \n \nday \n18 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nApproval \n? \n \nmonth \n08 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nComment \n \n \nyear \n26 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nATM \n? \n \nQA \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nTender \n \n \nIssued By \nVL \n \n \n